Bruce schrieb:
> 
> Stanislav Traykov <stanislavNoeZpam@tortoises.org> deserves a cookie for saying:
> > S. wrote on 09/24/2003 10:09 AM:
> >> Yep. Any IDs the character loses, stay lost until they're re-identified
> >> within the context of the game.

Which can consist of simply re-naming the objects in question to the
remembered identities...

[Amnesia partially destroys all sorts of item and place knowledge
acquired]
>  Meaning, IMO, that all #namings on any forgotten objects
> should be wiped as well.

Questionable - if you assume that the individual namings are made by
actually scratching something into the surface of the item with a
sharp object, or if they're in some other way equivalent to actually
sticking a post-it note on the named object, it'd be extremely
illogical if amnesia  removed such very material aide-memoires. 

Of course, if individual naming is physical it is a bit strange that
_un-individual_ naming information apparently is only kept inside the
brain, where it simply isn't safe.

> In the current situation, where #namings
> remain, I consider any #namings designed to circumvent amnesia or mind
> flayer attacks to be in poor taste at best and cheating at worst.

Say, if you make such notes _in the game_, i.e. scratch some key
identities into the floor with a hard gem on a particularly important
level - would you consider it cheating to actually go to, say, the up
staircase on the Castle level and read what's engraved into the floor
there?

It would still be an action that only has a use as mind flayer
insurance, but it would achieve this by game-internal
"outside-of-the-brain" information storage and making mind flayers
less harmful is a sensible thing to do from an in-game point of view.

As long as the DevTeam doesn't allow individual #namings to be wiped
out by amnesia, i assume that such information is stored by visibly
marking the object and thus naturally immune to amnesia attacks; using
this to circumvent amnesiac identity loss is in the current state of
the game _not cheating_; at worst it can be considered an abuse -
exploiting a shortcoming of the program.

